00:09:22,000 --> 00:09:24,000
so let's get started, shall we

193
00:09:24,000 --> 00:09:27,000
Scout Master's Ship in seven minutes

194
00:09:27,000 --> 00:09:29,000
or less.

195
00:09:35,000 --> 00:09:38,000
Well, sometimes it seems we're doing all the right things,

196
00:09:38,000 --> 00:09:41,000
but the results we're hoping for don't materialize.

197
00:09:41,000 --> 00:09:44,000
When Scout leaders grow frustrated with their work,

198
00:09:44,000 --> 00:09:47,000
it's usually because we're making one or more

199
00:09:47,000 --> 00:09:49,000
of the following mistakes.

200
00:09:49,000 --> 00:09:51,000
Number one is that we can tend

201
00:09:51,000 --> 00:09:53,000
to become a little over-involved-

202
00:09:53,000 --> 00:09:56,000
You've heard the term helicopter applied to parents

203
00:09:56,000 --> 00:09:58,000
and teachers and Scout leaders

204
00:09:58,000 --> 00:10:01,000
who are a little over-involved in their children's lives.

205
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:03,000
Scout leaders can over-plan.

206
00:10:03,000 --> 00:10:06,000
we can seek to control too many of the variables

207
00:10:06,000 --> 00:10:08,000
and we can reduce the scouting experience

208
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:11,000
to something more like a carefully guided tour

209
00:10:11,000 --> 00:10:13,000
rather than an open-ended adventure.

210
00:10:13,000 --> 00:10:15,000
We can become too driven by the concept

211
00:10:15,000 --> 00:10:19,000
of educating Scouts to the exclusion of real growth.

212
00:10:19,000 --> 00:10:21,000
Our honest good intentions sometimes

213
00:10:21,000 --> 00:10:23,000
cause exactly the opposite results

214
00:10:23,000 --> 00:10:24,000
that we were hoping for.

215
00:10:24,000 --> 00:10:26,000
In our effort to protect our Scouts

216
00:10:26,000 --> 00:10:28,000
from uncertainty and adversity

217
00:10:28,000 --> 00:10:30,000
and to optimize the productive use of their time

218
00:10:30,000 --> 00:10:33,000
and to develop balanced and healthy personalities,

219
00:10:33,000 --> 00:10:35,000
we can undermine some important

220
00:10:35,000 --> 00:10:37,000
natural developmental processes

221
00:10:37,000 --> 00:10:40,000
that actually drive the results we're looking for.

222
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:43,000
Number two is we can become very risk-averse.

223
00:10:43,000 --> 00:10:46,000
Our culture attempts to calculate and control risk-

224
00:10:46,000 --> 00:10:48,000
and you know everybody does,

225
00:10:48,000 --> 00:10:49,000
All cultures do-

226
00:10:49,000 --> 00:10:51,000
But several factors

227
00:10:51,000 --> 00:10:53,000
have distorted our perception of risk.

228
00:10:53,000 --> 00:10:57,000
We have access to information on a scale

229
00:10:57,000 --> 00:11:00,000
I would have to say that is just unprecedented

230
00:11:00,000 --> 00:11:01,000
in human history,

231
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:04,000
but our ability to sort through that information

232
00:11:04,000 --> 00:11:07,000
individually hasn't really grown proportionally.

233
00:11:07,000 --> 00:11:09,000
Fear and foreboding

234
00:11:09,000 --> 00:11:11,000
and highly attenuated emotions

235
00:11:11,000 --> 00:11:13,000
are in the news all the time,

236
00:11:13,000 --> 00:11:15,000
They're in entertainment all the time,

237
00:11:15,000 --> 00:11:17,000
And new media just floods our screens

238
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:19,000
and airways with this.

239
00:11:19,000 --> 00:11:21,000
Now, I'm not suggesting that this is some kind

240
00:11:21,000 --> 00:11:23,000
of coordinated conspiracy or anything like that.

241
00:11:23,000 --> 00:11:25,000
It's just human nature.

242
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:27,000
We simply don't get a balanced

243
00:11:27,000 --> 00:11:29,000
journalistic report about a child being abducted

244
00:11:29,000 --> 00:11:32,000
or abused or some kind of accident.

245
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:35,000
We end up being there watching the events unfold

246
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:37,000
hour upon hour,

247
00:11:37,000 --> 00:11:39,000
And we just don't see the story once.

248
00:11:39,000 --> 00:11:41,000
We see it hundreds of times.

249
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:43,000
If it gains enough attention,

250
00:11:43,000 --> 00:11:45,000
we're likely to see it dramatized

251
00:11:45,000 --> 00:11:47,000
in painful, lurid detail

252
00:11:47,000 --> 00:11:49,000
on a television show or in a movie,

253
00:11:49,000 --> 00:11:51,000
And communicating emotions on a visceral level

254
00:11:51,000 --> 00:11:53,000
is, in many ways,

255
00:11:53,000 --> 00:11:55,000
you know, the goal of everything

256
00:11:55,000 --> 00:11:57,000
that we do as humans.

257
00:11:57,000 --> 00:11:59,000
We want people to understand the way we feel,

258
00:11:59,000 --> 00:12:01,000
and we want people to understand

259
00:12:01,000 --> 00:12:03,000
the way things happen.

260
00:12:03,000 --> 00:12:05,000
The problem is, we've become so good at doing it.

261
00:12:05,000 --> 00:12:07,000
we can mistake emotion

262
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:09,000
for information.

263
00:12:09,000 --> 00:12:11,000
These messages teach us mistakenly

264
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:13,000
that all risk is bad,

265
00:12:13,000 --> 00:12:15,000
and our reaction is to reduce or eliminate

266
00:12:15,000 --> 00:12:17,000
risk in our children's lives

267
00:12:17,000 --> 00:12:19,000
in an attempt to make them safe and predictable.

268
00:12:19,000 --> 00:12:21,000
We find out soon enough, though,

269
00:12:21,000 --> 00:12:23,000
that it's really impossible to do

270
00:12:23,000 --> 00:12:25,000
So. we resolve to control

271
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:27,000
more and more of the variables,

272
00:12:27,000 --> 00:12:29,000
and this is more damaging

273
00:12:29,000 --> 00:12:31,000
than protective.

274
00:12:31,000 --> 00:12:33,000
If parents do this, and scout leaders

275
00:12:33,000 --> 00:12:35,000
do it too,

276
00:12:35,000 --> 00:12:37,000
Failure is that we misunderstand

277
00:12:37,000 --> 00:12:39,000
the place of failure.

278
00:12:39,000 --> 00:12:41,000
We've become so concerned about success

279
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:43,000
that we all have but eliminated

280
00:12:43,000 --> 00:12:45,000
the risk of failure in many aspects

281
00:12:45,000 --> 00:12:47,000
of our children's lives.

282
00:12:47,000 --> 00:12:49,000
Failure is actually good.

283
00:12:49,000 --> 00:12:51,000
Failure builds resilience and character.

284
00:12:51,000 --> 00:12:53,000
Failure is not the enemy.

285
00:12:53,000 --> 00:12:55,000
The enemy is shame

286
00:12:55,000 --> 00:12:57,000
and anger

287
00:12:57,000 --> 00:12:59,000
and discouragement

288
00:12:59,000 --> 00:13:01,000
and disappointment in reaction

289
00:13:01,000 --> 00:13:03,000
to things that don't go right.

290
00:13:03,000 --> 00:13:05,000
Our scouts need the latitude

291
00:13:05,000 --> 00:13:07,000
to attempt things and fail at them.

292
00:13:07,000 --> 00:13:09,000
It's fine.

293
00:13:09,000 --> 00:13:11,000
Whether or not our children

294
00:13:11,000 --> 00:13:13,000
and our scouts learn from failure

295
00:13:13,000 --> 00:13:15,000
hinges on our reaction to failure.

296
00:13:15,000 --> 00:13:17,000
We probably all recall

297
00:13:17,000 --> 00:13:19,000
some landmark in our childhood

298
00:13:19,000 --> 00:13:21,000
experience where we failed

299
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:23,000
and the adults around us

300
00:13:23,000 --> 00:13:25,000
reacted with anger or shame.

301
00:13:25,000 --> 00:13:27,000
We don't want our own children

302
00:13:27,000 --> 00:13:29,000
to experience this sort of thing,

303
00:13:29,000 --> 00:13:31,000
so we either rescue them before they fail

304
00:13:31,000 --> 00:13:33,000
or overcompensate by controlling

305
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:35,000
the natural consequences of the failure.

306
00:13:35,000 --> 00:13:37,000
How we react to failure

307
00:13:37,000 --> 00:13:39,000
is much, much more important

308
00:13:39,000 --> 00:13:41,000
than rescuing our children from it.

309
00:13:41,000 --> 00:13:43,000
Number four:

310
00:13:43,000 --> 00:13:45,000
scout leaders tend to emphasize

311
00:13:45,000 --> 00:13:47,000
rewards and we tend to lack

312
00:13:47,000 --> 00:13:49,000
objectivity.

313
00:13:49,000 --> 00:13:51,000
Scouting is not just a system

314
00:13:51,000 --> 00:13:53,000
of challenges and rewards,

315
00:13:53,000 --> 00:13:55,000
A list of things to do to get a badge.

316
00:13:55,000 --> 00:13:57,000
It's a journey through

317
00:13:57,000 --> 00:13:59,000
these important developmental

318
00:13:59,000 --> 00:14:01,000
experiences.

319
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:03,000
We know success in life is not

320
00:14:03,000 --> 00:14:05,000
a simple metric comparison

321
00:14:05,000 --> 00:14:07,000
reduced to winning and losing right.

322
00:14:07,000 --> 00:14:09,000
Our problem is

323
00:14:09,000 --> 00:14:11,000
that in our parenting and leadership,

324
00:14:11,000 --> 00:14:13,000
we can make it seem that everything

325
00:14:13,000 --> 00:14:15,000
a child or a scout does

326
00:14:15,000 --> 00:14:17,000
is measured and compared to others.

327
00:14:17,000 --> 00:14:19,000
When this becomes the norm,

328
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:21,000
parents and leaders become cheerleaders

329
00:14:21,000 --> 00:14:23,000
instead of coaches.

330
00:14:23,000 --> 00:14:25,000
Now, our children and our scouts

331
00:14:25,000 --> 00:14:27,000
both need encouragement

332
00:14:27,000 --> 00:14:29,000
as either undeserved

333
00:14:29,000 --> 00:14:31,000
or disingenuous.

334
00:14:31,000 --> 00:14:33,000
Coaches, on the other hand, are pretty objective.

335
00:14:33,000 --> 00:14:35,000
They help you find

336
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:37,000
your strengths and weaknesses and they give you

337
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:39,000
the tools to work with those strengths

338
00:14:39,000 --> 00:14:41,000
and weaknesses.

339
00:14:41,000 --> 00:14:43,000
Cheerleaders- not so objective.

340
00:14:43,000 --> 00:14:45,000
They're always basically telling you

341
00:14:45,000 --> 00:14:47,000
about how awesome you are,

342
00:14:47,000 --> 00:14:49,000
Even when things aren't going very well.

343
00:14:49,000 --> 00:14:51,000
parents and scout leaders need

344
00:14:51,000 --> 00:14:53,000
to balance their coaching and cheerleading

345
00:14:53,000 --> 00:14:55,000
to achieve real success

346
00:14:55,000 --> 00:14:57,000
and real growth,

347
00:14:57,000 --> 00:14:59,000
Those things that we're really driving at.

348
00:14:59,000 --> 00:15:01,000
They must have a studied

349
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:03,000
and consistent objectivity.

350
00:15:03,000 --> 00:15:05,000
Our children

351
00:15:05,000 --> 00:15:07,000
don't grow, our scouts don't grow

352
00:15:07,000 --> 00:15:09,000
and achieve because they are awesome-

353
00:15:09,000 --> 00:15:11,000
Okay,

354
00:15:11,000 --> 00:15:13,000
Even though

355
00:15:13,000 --> 00:15:15,000
they are-

356
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:17,000
But they grow and achieve

357
00:15:17,000 --> 00:15:19,000
because they learn to try

358
00:15:19,000 --> 00:15:21,000
to overcome challenges,

359
00:15:21,000 --> 00:15:23,000
to learn from failures

360
00:15:23,000 --> 00:15:25,000
and learn to be persistent.

361
00:15:25,000 --> 00:15:27,000
How do we avoid some of these common mistakes?

362
00:15:27,000 --> 00:15:29,000
by balancing our leadership.

363
00:15:29,000 --> 00:15:31,000
The overly effusive cheerleader

364
00:15:31,000 --> 00:15:33,000
and the overly critical

365
00:15:33,000 --> 00:15:35,000
taskmaster

366
00:15:35,000 --> 00:15:37,000
are both ends of

367
00:15:37,000 --> 00:15:39,000
an unbalanced spectrum.

368
00:15:39,000 --> 00:15:41,000
Balancing our leadership style

369
00:15:41,000 --> 00:15:43,000
is a never-ending challenge

370
00:15:43,000 --> 00:15:45,000
and one that we should be working always

371
00:15:45,000 --> 00:15:47,000
to improve. So here's some ideas

372
00:15:47,000 --> 00:15:49,000
that you may think about.

373
00:15:49,000 --> 00:15:51,000
Risk is not always dangerous.

374
00:15:51,000 --> 00:15:53,000
Surely, some risks are unacceptable,

375
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:55,000
but not all risks are dangerous.

376
00:15:55,000 --> 00:15:57,000
We have ample program guides

377
00:15:57,000 --> 00:15:59,000
and rules that define the line

378
00:15:59,000 --> 00:16:01,000
between acceptable and unacceptable risk.

379
00:16:01,000 --> 00:16:03,000
Scouts need the latitude

380
00:16:03,000 --> 00:16:05,000
to choose. Life is all about choices.

381
00:16:05,000 --> 00:16:07,000
You choose to do something

382
00:16:07,000 --> 00:16:09,000
or to be something,

383
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:11,000
But shaping and controlling every choice

384
00:16:11,000 --> 00:16:13,000
to guarantee that it's a good one

385
00:16:13,000 --> 00:16:15,000
is not a balanced approach,

386
00:16:15,000 --> 00:16:17,000
Neither is having no control

387
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:19,000
over that whatsoever.

388
00:16:19,000 --> 00:16:21,000
Every game has a field of play

389
00:16:21,000 --> 00:16:23,000
and has a set of rules,

390
00:16:23,000 --> 00:16:25,000
and so does scouting.

391
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:27,000
Some choices in scouting are simply out of bounds.

392
00:16:27,000 --> 00:16:29,000
Some choices in life are out of bounds,

393
00:16:29,000 --> 00:16:31,000
but if we eliminate possibility

394
00:16:31,000 --> 00:16:33,000
of all the out of bounds choices,

395
00:16:33,000 --> 00:16:35,000
we're not creating people with a resilient

396
00:16:35,000 --> 00:16:37,000
character

397
00:16:37,000 --> 00:16:39,000
or people who are able to make good choices.

398
00:16:39,000 --> 00:16:41,000
I think another thing we can do

399
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:43,000
is have a lot of empathy,

400
00:16:43,000 --> 00:16:45,000
and we need to have balanced empathy.

401
00:16:45,000 --> 00:16:47,000
We're in danger of forgetting that our scouts

402
00:16:47,000 --> 00:16:49,000
and think most adults are unerringly

403
00:16:49,000 --> 00:16:51,000
sure of themselves

404
00:16:51,000 --> 00:16:53,000
that we don't question or regret our own actions.

405
00:16:53,000 --> 00:16:55,000
Letting them know

406
00:16:55,000 --> 00:16:57,000
that we've fallen short at times

407
00:16:57,000 --> 00:16:59,000
and that we've recovered

408
00:16:59,000 --> 00:17:01,000
can help them better address

409
00:17:01,000 --> 00:17:03,000
their own difficulties.

410
00:17:03,000 --> 00:17:05,000
We need to be objective.

411
00:17:05,000 --> 00:17:07,000
Our scouts are awesome.

412
00:17:07,000 --> 00:17:09,000
Our children are awesome. We know it.

413
00:17:09,000 --> 00:17:11,000
We know how intelligent they are

414
00:17:11,000 --> 00:17:13,000
and what vast potential they have.

415
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:15,000
Of course, we know they can do great things,

416
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:17,000
but our greatness comes from hard work,

417
00:17:17,000 --> 00:17:19,000
not from just being awesome.

418
00:17:19,000 --> 00:17:21,000
Help them understand

419
00:17:21,000 --> 00:17:23,000
that being talented

420
00:17:23,000 --> 00:17:25,000
and skilled is only part of the equation.

421
00:17:25,000 --> 00:17:27,000
The rest of it

422
00:17:27,000 --> 00:17:29,000
is up to them.

423
00:17:29,000 --> 00:17:31,000
Once we have that objectivity,

424
00:17:31,000 --> 00:17:33,000
we really need balanced reactions.

425
00:17:33,000 --> 00:17:35,000
We need to avoid being angry

426
00:17:35,000 --> 00:17:37,000
and we need to be encouraging

427
00:17:37,000 --> 00:17:39,000
and empathetic and understanding.

428
00:17:39,000 --> 00:17:41,000
We need to balance rewards.

429
00:17:41,000 --> 00:17:43,000
Every achievement is certainly a step forward

430
00:17:43,000 --> 00:17:45,000
and every victory ought to be celebrated,

431
00:17:45,000 --> 00:17:47,000
but it doesn't always have to be

432
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:49,000
a tick or tape parade down Main Street.

433
00:17:49,000 --> 00:17:51,000
A few words of honest praise

434
00:17:51,000 --> 00:17:53,000
mean a lot more

435
00:17:53,000 --> 00:17:55,000
than that kind of showy

436
00:17:55,000 --> 00:17:57,000
over excess.

437
00:17:57,000 --> 00:17:59,000
And finally, we have to understand

438
00:17:59,000 --> 00:18:01,000
that failure is not a bad thing

439
00:18:01,000 --> 00:18:03,000
and it's a powerful teacher.

440
00:18:03,000 --> 00:18:05,000
We know that achievement comes

441
00:18:05,000 --> 00:18:07,000
more through hard work

442
00:18:07,000 --> 00:18:09,000
than the force of somebody's personality

443
00:18:09,000 --> 00:18:11,000
or awesomeness.

444
00:18:11,000 --> 00:18:13,000
In the same way,

445
00:18:13,000 --> 00:18:15,000
every failure is not caused

446
00:18:15,000 --> 00:18:17,000
by a lack of personality or character.

447
00:18:17,000 --> 00:18:19,000
Our job is to lend

448
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:21,000
every achievement and every failure

449
00:18:21,000 --> 00:18:23,000
that objectivity

450
00:18:23,000 --> 00:18:25,000
that will help our children

451
00:18:25,000 --> 00:18:27,000
and our scouts grow into useful,

452
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:29,000
important human beings.

453
00:18:41,000 --> 00:18:43,000
This is Cliff Jacobson,

454
00:18:43,000 --> 00:18:45,000
and you're listening to the

455
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:47,000
Scoutmaster podcast

456
00:18:47,000 --> 00:18:49,000
with Clark Greene

457
00:19:03,000 --> 00:19:05,000
Email. that is folks,

458
00:19:05,000 --> 00:19:07,000
And here's an answer to one of your emails.

459
00:19:11,000 --> 00:19:13,000
So

460
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:15,000
let's see,

461
00:19:15,000 --> 00:19:17,000
I had this email

462
00:19:17,000 --> 00:19:19,000
says I'm a scoutmaster

463
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:21,000
with a troop of 14 scouts

464
00:19:21,000 --> 00:19:23,000
and I was planning

465
00:19:23,000 --> 00:19:25,000
on trying to go to some of the local

466
00:19:25,000 --> 00:19:27,000
churches and talk with their congregations

467
00:19:27,000 --> 00:19:29,000
about scouting.

468
00:19:29,000 --> 00:19:31,000
I thought it would be a great way to spread the word

469
00:19:31,000 --> 00:19:33,000
about our troop to the community

470
00:19:33,000 --> 00:19:35,000
and hopefully recruit a few new scouts.

471
00:19:35,000 --> 00:19:37,000
but a lot of the pastors I've contacted

472
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:39,000
have not been very receptive to my idea.

473
00:19:39,000 --> 00:19:41,000
Frankly, that's the opposite reaction

474
00:19:41,000 --> 00:19:43,000
to what I was expecting.

475
00:19:43,000 --> 00:19:45,000
I understand why the local school district

476
00:19:45,000 --> 00:19:47,000
doesn't give us access,

477
00:19:47,000 --> 00:19:49,000
but I expected local churches to maybe

478
00:19:49,000 --> 00:19:51,000
be a little more welcoming.

479
00:19:51,000 --> 00:19:53,000
Do you have any other ideas where I might go

480
00:19:53,000 --> 00:19:55,000
to recruit new members?

481
00:19:55,000 --> 00:19:57,000
Well, I gotta tell you that going out

482
00:19:57,000 --> 00:19:59,000
and speaking to a church group

483
00:19:59,000 --> 00:20:01,000
or a school group or something like that,

484
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:03,000
I'm not going to say that it's

485
00:20:03,000 --> 00:20:05,000
unaffective and it's not a good way

486
00:20:05,000 --> 00:20:07,000
to spend time. I think that it can

487
00:20:07,000 --> 00:20:09,000
be a job of recruiting done,

488
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:11,000
but I wouldn't focus on that quite so much.

489
00:20:11,000 --> 00:20:13,000
If you go to another community group

490
00:20:13,000 --> 00:20:15,000
and you say I would like to come

491
00:20:15,000 --> 00:20:17,000
and talk about my community group,

492
00:20:17,000 --> 00:20:19,000
well that might be seen as

493
00:20:19,000 --> 00:20:21,000
kind of treading on their turf a little bit,

494
00:20:21,000 --> 00:20:23,000
especially with churches, with youth programs

495
00:20:23,000 --> 00:20:25,000
and things. They may welcome you

496
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:27,000
and they may not. I wouldn't be surprised

497
00:20:27,000 --> 00:20:29,000
if they don't. Here's my simple

498
00:20:29,000 --> 00:20:31,000
recruiting formula. If you

499
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:33,000
distribute a thousand pamphlets

500
00:20:33,000 --> 00:20:35,000
or handouts or something like that,

501
00:20:35,000 --> 00:20:37,000
you maybe get three or four potential scouts

502
00:20:37,000 --> 00:20:39,000
to attend a meeting-

503
00:20:39,000 --> 00:20:41,000
Three or four out of a thousand.

504
00:20:41,000 --> 00:20:43,000
If you go and you speak to a group of a

505
00:20:43,000 --> 00:20:45,000
hundred people, you maybe get

506
00:20:45,000 --> 00:20:47,000
one or two. If a scout

507
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:49,000
goes and talks to five

508
00:20:49,000 --> 00:20:51,000
of his friends, one of them is going to come

509
00:20:51,000 --> 00:20:53,000
to a scout meeting.

510
00:20:53,000 --> 00:20:55,000
If parents talk to other

511
00:20:55,000 --> 00:20:57,000
parents- let's say they talk to

512
00:20:57,000 --> 00:20:59,000
five or ten other sets of parents-

513
00:20:59,000 --> 00:21:01,000
you're going to get two or three.

514
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:03,000
you're going to get two or three

515
00:21:03,000 --> 00:21:05,000
families who will send scouts to a meeting.

516
00:21:05,000 --> 00:21:07,000
So if you just look at the ratios involved-

517
00:21:07,000 --> 00:21:09,000
and hey, the ratios I'm giving you are

518
00:21:09,000 --> 00:21:11,000
just kind of based on a seat of the parents,

519
00:21:11,000 --> 00:21:13,000
estimate from my own experiences,

520
00:21:13,000 --> 00:21:15,000
because I've tried all of those things

521
00:21:15,000 --> 00:21:17,000
And the gold standard for recruiting

522
00:21:17,000 --> 00:21:19,000
is scouts talking to their friends

523
00:21:19,000 --> 00:21:21,000
and parents talking to other

524
00:21:21,000 --> 00:21:23,000
parents, And it's hard to tell

525
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:25,000
which one is the most effective, but I know

526
00:21:25,000 --> 00:21:27,000
that parents talking to other parents

527
00:21:27,000 --> 00:21:29,000
is maybe just a little bit

528
00:21:29,000 --> 00:21:31,000
stronger than scouts talking to

529
00:21:31,000 --> 00:21:33,000
their friends who aren't in scouting. If I

530
00:21:33,000 --> 00:21:35,000
was in the position of really wanting

531
00:21:35,000 --> 00:21:37,000
to build up the troop and recruit a whole

532
00:21:37,000 --> 00:21:39,000
lot of guys, I would spend my time

533
00:21:39,000 --> 00:21:41,000
promoting

534
00:21:41,000 --> 00:21:43,000
those actions that have the

535
00:21:43,000 --> 00:21:45,000
best ratio of effort to results.

536
00:21:45,000 --> 00:21:47,000
So let's get the scouts to talk

537
00:21:47,000 --> 00:21:49,000
to scouts. Let's get our parents talking

538
00:21:49,000 --> 00:21:51,000
to parents and see what happened. I got

539
00:21:51,000 --> 00:21:53,000
this email from a scout

540
00:21:53,000 --> 00:21:55,000
leader who said: I'm a little confused

541
00:21:55,000 --> 00:21:57,000
about this because our scout

542
00:21:57,000 --> 00:21:59,000
master distributed this memo

543
00:21:59,000 --> 00:22:01,000
after our last camping trip

544
00:22:01,000 --> 00:22:03,000
And the memo says: on our last

545
00:22:03,000 --> 00:22:05,000
camp out, several scouts were sleeping alone

546
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:07,000
in their own tents.

547
00:22:07,000 --> 00:22:09,000
Sleeping alone in your own tent is a no-no.

548
00:22:09,000 --> 00:22:11,000
It's against the buddy system and youth protection.

549
00:22:11,000 --> 00:22:13,000
Please don't do this

550
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:15,000
in the future. If there's an

